  <!-- § 10 what this measures: evergreen -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The World Bank's Poverty and Inequality Platform takes each national household consumption survey, converts every household's spending per person into 2021 purchasing-power dollars a day using India's price index and the International Comparison Program's conversion factor, and counts the people below each line. It also publishes the distribution itself: the mean and median, the Gini index, and the share of consumption going to each tenth of the population.</p>
      <p>For India the surveys are the NSS consumer expenditure rounds from 1977-78 to 2011-12 and the Household Consumption Expenditure Surveys (HCES) of 2022-23 and 2023-24, run by the National Statistics Office. They measure what households spend and consume, including food grown at home, not what they earn. The World Bank, not the Government of India, sets the lines.</p>
      <p>Consumption is measured per person, with no allowance for the fact that children need less than adults or that a family of five shares one roof. Each PIP release can revise past estimates, for example when new price data or population figures arrive.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 11 misreadings: evergreen -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>How to misread these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li><span class="no">1</span><div><h3>"$3 a day is ₹250, so the poor live on ₹250 a day."</h3><p>The line is in purchasing-power dollars, not market dollars. At Indian prices it comes to a few dozen rupees a day per person, as the plate on lines shows.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">2</span><div><h3>"Poverty collapsed between 2011-12 and 2022-23."</h3><p>Partly. The two figures come from surveys with different questionnaires, and the redesigned survey records more consumption. Some of the fall is real, some is the new ruler; the World Bank does not join the two.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">3</span><div><h3>"Extreme poverty is over, so nobody in India is poor."</h3><p>The $3.00 line is a floor set for the poorest countries. By the line typical of countries at India's income level, $4.20, a much larger share is poor, and by the $8.30 line most Indians are.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">4</span><div><h3>"Inequality is falling: the Gini is the lowest ever."</h3><p>That is consumption inequality, measured by a survey that rarely reaches the richest households. Income and wealth inequality are different measures, from other sources, and can move the other way.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">5</span><div><h3>"The World Bank's yearly figures are survey results."</h3><p>Only survey years are. The figures for other years, including all of 2012 to 2021 and every year after the last survey, are model estimates built from national accounts.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

